BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T154133Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-philosophy-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T154133Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Philosophy Competition: First Edition
DESCRIPTION:The AI Philosophy Competition\, organised by philosophers Zacha
 ry Goodsell and Elliott Thornley and funded by Forethought Research\, aims
  to gauge the philosophical capabilities of AI in a way transparent to the
  philosophical community. Entrants submit up to three original essays on a
 ny philosophical topic\; essays must be primarily AI-generated\, with some
  human guidance allowed\, and each entry must include a methodology report
  explaining how the essay was produced. Essays are capped at 6\,000 words 
 each and may not be previously published or under review elsewhere\; entra
 nts consent to online publication and may choose anonymity. Judging is bli
 nd to methodology and authorship and evaluates the quality and originality
  of the philosophical content (style is not graded)\; the judging committe
 e includes David Chalmers\, John Hawthorne\, Herman Cappelen\, Cian Dorr a
 nd other academic philosophers. PRIZES: USD 3\,000 / 2\,000 / 1\,000 for t
 he top three essays\, plus a USD 5\,000 creative methodology pool. Finalis
 ts announced early December 2026\, results end of January 2027. DEADLINE: 
 31 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Zachary Goodsell and Elliott Thornley\, 
 funded by Forethought Research\nLocation: Online submission via OpenReview
  (preferred) or Google Forms\; open internationally.\nAward: USD 3\,000 fi
 rst prize\, USD 2\,000 second\, USD 1\,000 third\, plus a USD 5\,000 pool 
 for creative methodology distributed at the judges' discretion (USD 11\,00
 0 total). No entry fee.\nDuration: Finalists announced early December 2026
 \; results announced end of January 2027.\n\nMore: https://www.zacharygood
 sell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.zacharygoodsell.com/ai-philosophy-competition?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online submission via OpenReview (preferred) or Google Forms\; ope
 n internationally.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lukas-work-in-progress-awards-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T154133Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261203
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261204
SUMMARY:Deadline: J. Anthony Lukas Work-in-Progress Awards 2027
DESCRIPTION:The J. Anthony Lukas Work-in-Progress Awards\, given jointly by
  Columbia Journalism School and the Nieman Foundation at Harvard\, are ope
 n for 2027 submissions. Two annual awards of USD 25\,000 each help writers
  complete a book-length work of narrative nonfiction on a topic of America
 n political or social concern\; books on science\, technology and media ac
 countability have won in past years. Applicants need a fully executed cont
 ract with a US-based publisher for the book in progress. There is no entry
  fee for the Work-in-Progress awards\; note that the Lukas Book Prize and 
 the Mark Lynton History Prize run on the same portal with the same deadlin
 e but carry a USD 75 entry fee. TO APPLY: submit via the Columbia Journali
 sm Awards Submittable portal. DEADLINE: 3 December 2026\, 11:59 PM ET.\n\n
 Organisation: Columbia Journalism School and the Nieman Foundation\nLocati
 on: USA. Requires a fully executed contract with a US-based publisher.\nAw
 ard: Two awards of USD 25\,000 each. No entry fee for the Work-in-Progress
  awards.\n\nMore: https://columbiajournalismawards.submittable.com/submit?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://columbiajournalismawards.submittable.com/submit?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:USA. Requires a fully executed contract with a US-based publisher.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cove-park-awarded-residencies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261102T154133Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261211
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261212
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cove Park Awarded Residencies 2027-2028 (Scotland\, UK\, 
 Central and South America and diasporas)
DESCRIPTION:Cove Park's biennial call for fully funded Awarded Residencies 
 at its 50-acre rural residency site in Argyll\, on the west coast of Scotl
 and\, programmed between May 2027 and December 2028. Residencies run from 
 2 weeks up to 12 weeks (average four weeks) and can be split across the tw
 o years around caring or work commitments\; international residents can co
 unt slow-travel time as part of the funded period. Open to artists\, creat
 ive practitioners\, writers\, translators and researchers in all art forms
  and at every career stage\, including collectives and collaborative group
 s\; no formal qualification needed. Priorities include work responding to 
 climate breakdown\, sound artists and musicians (new Sound Studio)\, and w
 ork on home\, place\, language (Scots\, Gaelic\, BSL)\, displacement and b
 elonging. No final presentation is required. ELIGIBILITY: based in the UK\
 , Central America or South America\; Central and South American diaspora a
 rtists based in Europe or North America\; or born in Scotland and living e
 lsewhere. Anyone in full- or part-time formal education on 11 December 202
 6 is not eligible. SUPPORT: GBP 775 per week fee\; travel allowance (Scotl
 and GBP 65 to 100 Central Belt\, up to GBP 200 Borders/Highlands/Islands\;
  rest of UK up to GBP 250\; Europe up to GBP 400\; other international up 
 to GBP 1\,250)\; materials up to GBP 150\; visa support\; private self-cat
 ering accommodation (suitable for collaborators and those attending with c
 hildren) and private studios. An Application Access Bursary (e.g. to pay a
  support worker) is available for applicants facing barriers due to disabi
 lity or neurodivergence\; request it from Alex Marrs by 30 November 2026. 
 Video or audio applications accepted. Applicants are asked not to use gene
 rative AI in their application. APPLY via the online form: 500-word statem
 ent\, 100-word bio\, CV\, up to 8 images or video/audio links or writing s
 amples. SELECTION: shortlisting by the programme team and former residents
 \; shortlist notified by 12 February 2027\; Zoom interviews the week of 22
  February 2027. Contact: Alex Marrs\, alex@covepark.org. DEADLINE: Friday 
 11 December 2026\, 5pm GMT.\n\nOrganisation: Cove Park\nLocation: Cove Par
 k\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, private self-cat
 ering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in the UK\, Central 
 or South America\, Central and South American diaspora artists based in Eu
 rope or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living anywhere. Student
 s in formal education are not eligible.\nAward: Fully funded: GBP 775 per 
 week fee (VAT inclusive\, freelance)\, travel allowance (Scotland GBP 65 t
 o 200\, rest of UK up to GBP 250\, Europe up to GBP 400\, other internatio
 nal up to GBP 1\,250)\, materials up to GBP 150 for studio-based practitio
 ners\, private accommodation and studio\, visa and access support. Applica
 tion Access Bursary available (request by 30 November 2026). No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: 2 to 12 weeks (average 4)\, between May 2027 and Decembe
 r 2028\n\nMore: https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cove Park\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, 
 private self-catering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in t
 he UK\, Central or South America\, Central and South American diaspora art
 ists based in Europe or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living a
 nywhere. Students in formal education are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
